.TH HOSTS 1 2026-07-27 "hosts @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.\" Written by hand: no generator, no conversion step.
.SH NAME
hosts \- a safe command\-line manager for /etc/hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hosts
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hosts
manages the entries of the system hosts file without opening it in an
editor.
The hosts file is critical for name resolution, so safety comes before
convenience: input is validated before anything is touched, and writes are
performed on a temporary file in the same directory, validated, and then
moved into place with an atomic rename that preserves owner, group and
permissions.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "ls [" options "] [" pattern ]
List the entries of the file.
With a
.IR pattern ,
only entries carrying a name that matches that shell glob are listed;
matching ignores case, and the pattern has to be quoted so that the shell
does not expand it first.
.RS
.PP
.B \-a
or
.B \-\-all
lists active and disabled entries,
.B \-\-disabled
only the disabled ones.
Entries inside the block section are left out unless
.B \-\-blocked
is given.
.RE
.TP
.BI "get " hostname
Print the addresses the hostname points at, one per line and nothing else,
so that the result can be captured directly.
Only active entries are considered, because a commented out entry takes no
part in name resolution.
The match is exact and ignores case.
Exits with 5 when the hostname is not in the file.
.TP
.BI "search " text
Find entries whose address, names or comment contain the text.
Matching is on substrings and ignores case.
Disabled entries are included.
Output is in the format of
.BR ls .
.TP
.B check
Lint the file and report what is wrong with it.
See
.B CHECK RULES
below.
.B \-\-strict
makes a warning fail as an error does, and
.B \-\-fix
repairs the findings that have only one possible repair.
.TP
.B export
Write the file to standard output.
Without
.B \-\-json
this reproduces it byte for byte, comments, blank lines and a missing final
newline included.
.TP
.BI "add " address " " hostname ...
Point one or more hostnames at an address.
Adding what is already there changes nothing and succeeds.
.RS
.PP
A name missing from a line that already carries the others, on the same
address, is added to it as an alias; names that are nowhere yet go on a new
line at the end of the file.
Names spread over several lines are refused, because merging lines is a
decision for you to make.
.PP
A name that already points somewhere else in the same address family is
refused, and
.B \-\-force
points it at the new address instead.
The same name on an IPv4 and on an IPv6 address is ordinary dual stack and is
never treated as a clash.
A disabled entry for the name is refused too:
.B on
is usually what was meant, and
.B \-\-force
replaces it.
.RE
.TP
.BI "rm " hostname | address
Given a hostname, take it off every line that carries it, active or disabled,
and drop a line left with nothing but its address.
When the name was the first on its line, the next one becomes the canonical
name.
Given an address, remove every line that points at it.
Exits with 5 when nothing matches, so that a typo does not pass for success.
.TP
.BI "on " hostname
Enable every disabled entry carrying the hostname.
.TP
.BI "off " hostname
Comment out every active entry carrying the hostname, keeping the line so that
.B on
can bring it back.
Every line carrying the name is disabled, not just one: turning off half of a
dual stack pair would leave the name resolving on the other family.
.TP
.B edit
Open the file in an editor, check what comes back, and install it atomically
after taking a backup.
The editor works on a copy, so the file is never left half written.
Errors stop it being installed, warnings are printed and let through.
Your work is never thrown away: with a terminal the editor opens again with
the findings shown, the way
.BR visudo (8)
does it, and without one the path of your copy is printed.
The editor is
.BR $VISUAL ,
then
.BR $EDITOR ,
then
.BR vi ,
and it runs with the locale you had rather than the one this program sets for
its own parsing.
.TP
.BI "import [" file ]
Merge entries from a file, or from standard input when no file is given.
The source is checked before anything is written, and one bad line makes the
whole import fail.
Only active entries are taken, and an entry is taken or left as a whole: one
that only partly overlaps what is already there is left alone and reported,
because a line half imported would put a name on two lines at once.
.TP
.BI "block " domain ...
Point domains at a sinkhole address so that they stop resolving.
With no argument the domains are read from standard input, one per line, since
a real blocklist does not fit on a command line.
Both
.B 0.0.0.0
and
.B ::
are used unless
.B \-\-ipv4\-only
says otherwise, or
.BI \-\-to " address"
names another one.
A domain that already has an entry outside the section is skipped with a
warning rather than making the whole command fail, deliberately unlike
.BR add .
See
.B BLOCK SECTION
below.
.TP
.B backup
Take a backup of the file.
This only reads the file, so it needs no privilege on it; what it needs is
somewhere to write the copy.
A backup that would be identical to the most recent one is not taken.
.TP
.B "backup ls"
List the backups already taken, most recent first, as four tab separated
fields: index, identifier, time, size in bytes.
.TP
.BI "restore [" id ]
Put a backup back in place, the most recent one by default.
.I id
is either the index shown by
.B "backup ls"
, where 1 is the most recent, or an identifier in full.
The content that is about to be replaced is backed up first, so a restore can
itself be undone.
Only the content is restored: ownership and permissions stay as they are now.
.TP
.BI "profile save " name
Keep the file as it is now under a name.
Saving only reads the file, so it needs no privilege on it.
Saving over a name that exists is refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.BI "profile load " name
Bring that state back, replacing what is in the file.
The content being replaced is kept first, so a load can be undone with
.BR restore .
.TP
.B "profile ls"
List the profiles, as three tab separated fields: name, time, size in bytes.
.TP
.BI "profile rm " name
Delete a profile.
This asks first, because nothing else holds a copy of it.
.TP
.BI "diff [" id ]
Compare the file with a backup, the most recent one by default.
Removed lines are what the backup holds, added lines what the file holds now.
Exits with 0 whether or not there are differences.
.TP
.B flush
Clear the cache of the system resolver.
Often there is nothing to clear, and that is not a failure: the C library has
no DNS cache of its own, so without a caching daemon in the path a change to
the file is already in effect.
Only flushes that cannot disturb anything are performed, namely
systemd-resolved and nscd; dnsmasq, unbound and BIND are reported with the
command that would reload them and left alone, because restarting a resolver
can drop the network of the machine.
.PP
Every command accepts
.B \-\-help
and describes itself.
.SH OPTIONS
These are accepted before and after the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " path"
Operate on a file other than
.IR /etc/hosts .
A symbolic link is followed, so that the write lands where it points instead
of replacing the link with a regular file.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Produce machine readable output.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen and write nothing.
The exit status is the one the real run would have had.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-backup
Skip the automatic backup taken before a change.
Not advisable.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Go ahead with something that would otherwise be refused.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Answer yes to the confirmations.
Without a terminal and without this option a confirmation is refused rather
than assumed.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Silence diagnostics.
The data a command was asked to produce is never suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print diagnostics about what is being done, on standard error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the program version and exit.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
Diagnostics go to standard error, data to standard output.
The text output of
.B ls
and
.B search
is four tab separated fields, always in this order and always this many,
on a terminal exactly as in a pipe:
.PP
.RS
address, names separated by a space, on or off, comment
.RE
.PP
Nothing is aligned, coloured or paginated, and no option changes the number
of fields, so
.B "cut \-f1"
is always the address.
.PP
The JSON schema carries a
.B version
field and is part of the public interface.
Bytes outside ASCII are passed through unchanged.
.SH CHECK RULES
An error is something the resolver will get wrong or silently ignore; a
warning is something untidy or merely suspect.
.PP
Errors:
.BR invalid\-line ,
a line that is not an address followed by a hostname;
.BR invalid\-ip ,
an address that does not parse;
.BR invalid\-hostname ,
a name breaking RFC 1123;
.BR control\-character ,
a control character in the line;
.BR duplicate\-entry ,
an earlier line that is identical.
.PP
Warnings:
.BR duplicate\-name ,
the name already points at that address on an earlier line;
.BR conflicting\-ip ,
the name points at a different address of the same family;
.BR nonstandard\-hostname ,
a name using an underscore;
.BR missing\-loopback " and " missing\-loopback6 ,
no active entry maps 127.0.0.1 or ::1 to localhost;
.BR missing\-trailing\-newline ,
the file does not end with a newline.
.PP
The same name on an IPv4 and on an IPv6 address is normal dual stack and is
not reported.
Rules that compare lines ignore disabled entries, which resolve nothing.
.PP
Findings are printed on standard output in the format compilers use, so that
the error parser of an editor can consume them directly:
.PP
.RS
.I file : line ": " severity ": " rule ": " message
.RE
.SS Fixing
.B \-\-fix
repairs the findings whose repair the finding itself forces, and only those:
.BR duplicate\-entry ,
by removing the later line, which changes nothing about what the file resolves;
.BR duplicate\-name ,
by taking the redundant name off the line, and dropping the line if that leaves
it with nothing but an address;
.BR missing\-loopback " and " missing\-loopback6 ,
by adding the entry above the first one the file already has;
.BR missing\-trailing\-newline ,
by adding the newline.
.PP
The rest are left alone, deliberately.
An address or a name that does not parse cannot be repaired without inventing
one.
.B conflicting\-ip
is the one that tempts: two lines send a name to two addresses, and which of
them is wanted is knowledge the file does not contain.
.B control\-character
is left because stripping bytes out of a line in silence would destroy the
evidence of what happened to it.
.PP
A finding that was fixed stops being reported, since it is no longer true of
the file and an editor jumping to the line would land on one that is now
correct or gone.
What is left is printed as usual and still decides the exit status.
Fixing takes a backup first, honours
.B \-\-dry\-run
and
.BR \-\-no\-backup ,
and asks before touching more than one line unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.SH WRITING
A change never rebuilds the file from what was parsed out of it.
It says which lines it replaces, which it deletes and what it appends, and
every other line is written back exactly as it was read.
Within a line the edit is surgical too: a name is removed together with one
run of adjacent whitespace, so that
.B "10.0.0.5    staging   staging.local"
loses an alias without having its columns rewritten.
The result is that the diff of a command shows what was asked for and nothing
else.
.PP
Every change is written to a temporary file in the directory of the target,
validated, and then moved into place with a rename.
The kernel performs a rename within one filesystem atomically, so after a
change the file holds either the whole of the old content or the whole of the
new one, never a mixture, and that holds through a crash or a power cut.
.PP
What cannot be promised is that the new content survives a power cut in the
moment right after the rename: bash cannot call
.BR fsync (2),
so the directory entry cannot be forced to disk.
The failure mode of that is finding the old file, which is to say a lost
change, never a damaged one.
.PP
Owner, group, permissions and SELinux context are carried over to the new
file before it is installed.
An extended ACL cannot be carried over by a rename, so a file that has one is
refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.PP
Writers are serialised with
.BR flock (1)
when it is available.
The lock is taken before the file is read and held until the new content is in
place, which is the part that matters: two writers that both read before
either writes each compute their change from the same starting point, and the
second rename then throws the first change away.
Serialising only the writes would prevent nothing, the rename having already
made those indivisible.
.PP
.B edit
holds that lock for as long as the editor is open, and another writer waits ten
seconds and then fails saying so.
Discarding one of the two changes instead would be worse.
.PP
Without
.BR flock (1)
writes are not serialised and a concurrent write can lose an update, though it
still cannot damage the file.
A lock built out of
.BR mkdir (1)
would have to guess whether a leftover lock belongs to a live process, and
guessing wrong leaves the tool stuck for good: worse than the problem it
solves.
.SH BLOCK SECTION
Blocked domains are kept together at the end of the file, between two markers:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# >>> hosts block >>>
# Managed by hosts(1): change it with "hosts block" and "hosts rm".
0.0.0.0	ads.example.com
::	ads.example.com
# <<< hosts block <<<
.EE
.RE
.PP
Marking the section off means a blocklist of tens of thousands of machine
written lines can be treated as one thing.
.B ls
leaves it out unless asked, so that the handful of entries someone actually
maintains are not buried under it, and
.B check
does not compare its lines with each other, since near identical names written
by a script would produce nothing but noise.
The per line rules still apply to it, and
.BR get ,
.B search
and
.B rm
treat what is inside exactly as they treat anything else: nothing about the
section is magic, and the markers go away with the last entry in it.
.SH BACKUPS
A backup is taken before every change unless
.B \-\-no\-backup
says otherwise.
Backups live in one directory per target file under
.IR /var/backups/hosts ,
each one a byte for byte copy with a sidecar of metadata beside it.
The copy is deliberately plain, so that in an emergency the file can be put
back with
.BR cp (1)
knowing nothing about this program.
.PP
The sidecar records the absolute path the backup was taken from, and
.B restore
refuses when that is not the file it is about to write.
Without that check a backup taken with
.B \-\-file
from a scratch file could later be written over
.IR /etc/hosts .
The sidecar also records a SHA-256 of the copy, which
.B restore
verifies, and which is what tells an unchanged file from a changed one when
deciding whether a new backup is worth taking.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is a snapshot of the whole file kept under a name, so that a known
state can be brought back later.
It is not an overlay: loading one replaces what is there.
.PP
A profile and a backup are the same object with different lifetimes.
Backups are taken automatically before every change and rotate away; profiles
are made on purpose and stay until they are deleted, which is why they live
apart, under
.IR /var/lib/hosts/profiles .
Everything that makes a backup safe applies unchanged: the copy is byte for
byte, the sidecar records the file it came from, and the checksum is verified
before anything is written.
.PP
A profile name becomes part of a filename, so it may hold only letters,
digits, dot, dash and underscore, may not begin with a dot or a dash, and is
at most 64 characters.
That is a question of safety rather than of tidiness.
.SH COMPLETION
Completions for bash and zsh are installed by
.BR "make install" .
.PP
Profile names and backup identifiers are always offered, since producing them
means listing a directory.
Hostnames are offered only while the file is small enough, because producing
them means reading and parsing it, and on a file holding a large blocklist
that takes seconds and would leave the shell looking as though it had hung.
The limit is 5000 lines and
.B HOSTS_COMPLETION_MAX_LINES
changes it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOSTS_COMPLETION_MAX_LINES
The largest file, in lines, for which the shell completion offers hostnames.
Defaults to 5000.
.TP
.B HOSTS_PROFILE_DIR
Where profiles are kept.
Defaults to
.IR /var/lib/hosts/profiles .
.TP
.B HOSTS_BACKUP_DIR
Where backups are kept.
Defaults to
.IR /var/backups/hosts .
.TP
.B HOSTS_KEEP_BACKUPS
How many backups to keep per file.
Defaults to 20.
The most recent is never removed, whatever this says.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hosts
The static table of hostname to address mappings, and the default target of
every command.
.TP
.I /var/backups/hosts
The backup store, one directory per target file.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hosts/profiles
The profile store, likewise one directory per target file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit codes are part of the public interface and keep their meaning for the
whole 1.x release line.
Values greater than or equal to 64 are reserved and never returned.
.TP
.B 0
Success, including an operation that changed nothing.
.TP
.B 1
Generic or unexpected failure, such as a target file that is not there.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown command, unknown option, or a missing argument.
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient permissions to read the file.
.TP
.B 4
Invalid input, or
.B check
found errors.
.TP
.B 5
The requested hostname or backup is not there.
.TP
.B 6
Refused because
.B \-\-force
was not given.
.TP
.B 7
The write was abandoned because the result could not be trusted, and nothing
was written.
.TP
.B 8
The operation was not confirmed.
.SH EXAMPLES
List every entry, then only the ones under a domain:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hosts ls
hosts ls '*.local'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Capture the address of a host:
.PP
.RS
.EX
address=$(hosts get staging)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Lint a candidate file in continuous integration, failing on warnings too:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hosts \-\-file ./hosts.new check \-\-strict
.EE
.RE
.PP
Point a hostname at a staging box, then move it and take it away:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo hosts add 10.0.0.5 staging staging.local
sudo hosts \-\-force add 10.0.0.9 staging staging.local
sudo hosts off staging
sudo hosts rm staging
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep a copy before editing by hand, see what changed, and go back:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo hosts backup
sudo hosts edit
hosts diff
sudo hosts restore \-\-yes
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep two named states of the file and move between them:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo hosts profile save work
sudo hosts profile load home
hosts profile ls
.EE
.RE
.PP
Load a downloaded blocklist, then see what is in it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
curl \-s https://example.com/domains.txt | sudo hosts block \-\-yes
hosts ls \-\-blocked | wc \-l
sudo hosts rm ads.example.com
.EE
.RE
.SH INSTALLATION
On Debian and Ubuntu, from the signed repository published alongside this
documentation:
.PP
.RS
.EX
curl \-fsSL https://n36l3c7.github.io/hosts-cli/apt/hosts-cli.asc \e
  | sudo gpg \-\-dearmor \-o /usr/share/keyrings/hosts-cli.gpg
echo 'deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hosts-cli.gpg] \e
  https://n36l3c7.github.io/hosts-cli/apt stable main' \e
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hosts-cli.list
sudo apt update && sudo apt install hosts-cli
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hosts (5),
.BR hostname (7),
.BR getent (1)
.PP
Full documentation is available on
.UR https://n36l3c7.github.io/hosts-cli/
the project website
.UE .
